{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel were in the wilderness, etc.</b> It is difficult: what does the verse come to tell us that Israel was in the wilderness? And it may be explained according to what they said in tractate Shabbat (Shabbat 96b), and this is their language: Rav Yehudah said in the name of Shmuel: the wood-gatherer was carrying four cubits in the public domain — end of quote. They also said in tractate Shabbat, chapter 1 (Shabbat 6a), and this is their language: The Tanna'im taught: streets and plazas, etc. — this is the public domain. And they raised a difficulty in the Talmud: but one should also count the wilderness, for it was taught in a baraita: What is the public domain? Streets, etc., and the wilderness. And Abaye resolved it: it is not difficult — here, at the time when Israel were dwelling in the wilderness; here, in the present era. And Rashi explained, and this is his language: At that time it was considered a public domain; in the present era it is not a place of passage for the multitudes — end of quote. Meaning, the baraita that taught 'and the wilderness' comes to tell us that if there are multitudes currently present in the wilderness regularly, it too is included in the public domain." "<b>And this</b> is what the verse said: when it wished to inform us of the transgression of the wood-gatherer, that he was carrying four cubits according to the opinion of Rav Yehudah, it prefaced by saying ״וַיִּהְיוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בַּמִּדְבָּר״ — meaning, it was through this that the desecration of the Sabbath was counted for him to incur the death penalty. But if Israel had not been in the wilderness, even though he was carrying four cubits there, he would not be liable, for it would not have the status of a public domain." "<b>According to</b> the opinion of the Rambam (Hilkhot Shabbat, ch. 14), according to what is heard from his words — from the fact that he counted the wilderness within the category of public domain and did not distinguish between when Israel are present and when they are not present — and I explained it in my work ״חֵפֶץ ה׳״ that I composed in the days of my youth, that the Rambam interprets the words of Abaye in this manner: ״Here, at the time when Israel were [in the wilderness]״ — meaning, the Tanna who mentioned the wilderness was speaking of that time, therefore he counted it as well; and the Tanna who did not mention it was speaking only of the present era, and in the present era those who walk in wildernesses are not present, and since they are not present he did not trouble to teach us its law. But it is not so that even in the present era the wilderness is a public domain, for if so we return to our difficulty along this path: why did the verse need to say ״וַיִּהְיוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בַּמִּדְבָּר״?" "<b>And it may be explained</b> further according to what they also said there (Shabbat 96b), and this is their language: In the mishna the Tanna taught that the wood-gatherer was uprooting — end of quote. And according to this, a difficulty arises regarding the words of the Tanna: for did not our Sages say (Ḥullin 88a) regarding the laws of covering the blood of a wild animal or bird, that one does not cover with desert dust because it is salty and does not promote growth — end of quote? If so, from where did plants grow in the wilderness for the wood-gatherer to uproot? Therefore the verse prefaced by saying <b>וַיִּהְיוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בַּמִּדְבָּר</b>, and they of blessed memory said (Bemidbar Rabbah, ch. 9) that through the well of Miriam there was irrigation, and it would bring up gardens and orchards, and if so plants grew in the wilderness to uproot. Also according to the opinion of Rav Aḥa bar Ya'akov who said there (Shabbat, ibid.) that he was gathering (me'ameir), the verse is also explained along this path." "<b>And they found a man, etc.</b> It uses the language of 'finding.' This may be explained according to the way they of blessed memory said (Yalkut Shim'oni) that Moshe said to them: Go out and see whether there is any man who desecrated the Sabbath, etc. — end of quote. Therefore it says ״וַיִּמְצְאוּ״ along the lines of the verse (Esther 2:23) ״וַיְבֻקַּשׁ הַדָּבָר וַיִּמָּצֵא״ — 'the matter was searched out and it was found.'" "<b>Gathering wood on the Sabbath day.</b> It placed the mention of the deed before the mention of the day, and did not say ״on the Sabbath day gathering wood״ — to say that the Sabbath was not hidden from him when he performed his deed. And if it had placed the mention of the Sabbath first, it would have been understood that they found him on the Sabbath day in the sense known to them; but from its saying ״gathering wood on the Sabbath day,״ the mention of the Sabbath reverts to the gatherer — that he knew it was Sabbath. And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sifrei) were clever in extracting this point — that he knew it was Sabbath — from the fact that it repeated in the subsequent verse ״gathering wood,״ which comes to say that after they informed him it was Sabbath he returned to gather. And according to our approach, the reason it repeated a second time ״gathering״ is to inform us that there was no error regarding the labor on his part, but rather they informed him that it is a labor for which one is liable, and this is its saying ״that man who gathered״ — meaning, he knew that he was performing a labor; and in this he had both intentionality regarding the Sabbath and intentionality regarding the labor."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {2 "The work חֵפֶץ ה׳ is the Or HaChaim's own earlier halakhic treatise, composed in his youth."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:10:11.813276101Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}